WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:08.220
The first Android walkthrough rendered a payload from a file. Nothing talked back.

2
00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:09.659
This one closes the loop.

3
00:00:09.659 --> 00:00:13.457
An agent streams a surface over the network, the renderer draws it

4
00:00:13.457 --> 00:00:17.256
the user edits and taps, and that action travels back to the agent

5
00:00:17.256 --> 00:00:19.328
which answers with the next surface.

6
00:00:19.328 --> 00:00:22.897
Three surfaces, two round trips, no layout code on the client.

7
00:00:22.897 --> 00:00:23.855
Here is the agent.

8
00:00:23.855 --> 00:00:28.010
Every surface it can send is written out in advance, right here in the source.

9
00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:30.459
Nothing is generated when the request arrives.

10
00:00:30.459 --> 00:00:37.170
That matters if you work somewhere that has to review and sign off on what the user sees: the structure is fixed and auditable

11
00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:38.502
and only the values move.

12
00:00:38.502 --> 00:00:44.520
Notice the form is sent as four separate messages rather than one, so the client can draw each piece as it lands.

13
00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.153
The client side is two loops.

14
00:00:46.153 --> 00:00:51.787
The first asks the agent for a surface and feeds each line into the processor the moment it arrives.

15
00:00:51.787 --> 00:00:53.815
The second collects outbound events.

16
00:00:53.815 --> 00:01:01.138
Every time the user triggers an action, the renderer emits one, and we post it straight back to the agent and stream the reply in.

17
00:01:01.138 --> 00:01:05.024
That is the entire integration. Everything else is the library's job.

18
00:01:05.024 --> 00:01:06.726
Watch the first surface arrive.

19
00:01:06.726 --> 00:01:11.609
The app starts with nothing, asks the agent, and the form assembles as the messages land.

20
00:01:11.609 --> 00:01:18.414
Title and description first, then the card of fields, then the button, and finally the data model that fills every field in.

21
00:01:18.414 --> 00:01:24.340
This is progressive rendering: the user sees structure immediately instead of waiting for the whole payload.

22
00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:25.298
Now the user acts.

23
00:01:25.298 --> 00:01:29.131
Ticking the box writes to the client data model through two-way binding.

24
00:01:29.131 --> 00:01:33.177
Tapping Review sends an event to the agent carrying that data model with it.

25
00:01:33.177 --> 00:01:38.713
The agent picks its review template, fills it with the values the client just sent, and streams it back.

26
00:01:38.713 --> 00:01:44.515
Look at the frequency row: it says Monthly, because the checkbox we ticked made the trip and came home again.

27
00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:52.052
Confirm sends the second event and the agent replies with a receipt it built at that moment, with a fresh reference number.

28
00:01:52.052 --> 00:01:57.370
Three surfaces have now been rendered natively, and the client never knew what any of them would look like.

29
00:01:57.370 --> 00:01:59.905
It only knew how to render whatever the agent sent.

30
00:01:59.905 --> 00:02:07.410
The reference number and the posting time were not in the client at all a second ago; they were composed by the agent and rendered natively on arrival.

31
00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:10.351
And this is the same conversation from the agent's side.

32
00:02:10.351 --> 00:02:17.127
One request for the opening surface, then two events arriving with the user's data attached, each answered with the next surface.

33
00:02:17.127 --> 00:02:21.067
Swap this agent for your own service and the client does not change at all.
